      "changes": {
        "problem": {
          "db_text": "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\nI. $4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\nII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+10=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\nIII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-6$",
          "checked_source_text": "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\nI. $4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+10=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-6$",
          "unified_diff": "--- \n+++ \n@@ -1,5 +1,7 @@\n Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n \n I. $4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n+\n II. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+10=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n+\n III. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-6$"
        },
        "worked_solution": {
          "db_text": "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=10$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=6$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation I has no solutions.",
          "checked_source_text": "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\nI. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=10$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=6$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation I has no solutions.",
          "unified_diff": "--- \n+++ \n@@ -1,8 +1,10 @@\n We first simplify the given equations, and get\n \n-- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n-- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=10$\n-- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=6$\n+I. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n+\n+II. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=10$\n+\n+III. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=6$\n \n Now, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n "
        }
